1
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:28,160
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:38,303
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:51,450
♪ ♪

4
00:01:15,843 --> 00:01:17,175
Good party, huh?

5
00:01:17,244 --> 00:01:19,944
Oh, yeah.

6
00:01:20,013 --> 00:01:22,573
Yeah, they always
seem to have a good time.

7
00:01:31,525 --> 00:01:33,458
Two olives here.

8
00:01:34,528 --> 00:01:35,705
Okay, there you go.

9
00:01:35,729 --> 00:01:36,828
See you in a minute.

10
00:01:36,897 --> 00:01:38,107
He's over there down the hall.

11
00:01:38,131 --> 00:01:40,691
Oh, I see him, thanks.

12
00:01:43,704 --> 00:01:45,824
Hi. Hi.

13
00:01:50,544 --> 00:01:53,478
Well, you did it again, Cal.

14
00:01:53,547 --> 00:01:54,924
You expected otherwise?

15
00:01:57,651 --> 00:01:59,796
Darling, she looks absolutely
gorgeous in that color.

16
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:01,798
Darling! Hi.

17
00:02:01,822 --> 00:02:04,423
Oh. Vodka martini,
dry, two olives.

18
00:02:04,491 --> 00:02:05,557
Thank you, sweetheart.

19
00:02:05,626 --> 00:02:07,169
Well, if I had all of this,

20
00:02:07,193 --> 00:02:08,805
I'd be willing to
admit I was 40.

21
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:11,875
I'm going to go check
on the champagne.

22
00:02:11,899 --> 00:02:14,165
Excuse me.

23
00:02:14,234 --> 00:02:17,814
Sweetie, all I can say is
I wish I'd seen him first.

24
00:02:17,838 --> 00:02:20,271
Thank God I did.

25
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:22,941
You know after Rick died, I
never thought that I could...

26
00:02:24,511 --> 00:02:26,611
But these past three
years have been wonderful.

27
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:29,214
I have never seen you happier.

28
00:02:29,282 --> 00:02:31,761
Mom also looks the best ever.

29
00:02:31,785 --> 00:02:34,853
Tony Morgen, you are smooth.

30
00:02:34,922 --> 00:02:37,455
Now, this handsome boy
should be out enjoying himself.

31
00:02:37,524 --> 00:02:39,703
- Who says I'm not?
- Amazing.

32
00:02:39,727 --> 00:02:41,994
Well, you know me,
always comfortable.

33
00:02:57,477 --> 00:02:59,477
Those things'll kill you.

34
00:02:59,546 --> 00:03:01,479
You wish.

35
00:03:02,816 --> 00:03:03,960
Party's going well, isn't it?

36
00:03:03,984 --> 00:03:05,650
Yeah, yeah.

37
00:03:05,719 --> 00:03:07,819
Mom's money makes
everything go well.

38
00:03:09,489 --> 00:03:10,956
I thought we had
an agreement, Tony?

39
00:03:11,024 --> 00:03:12,223
No arguments tonight.

40
00:03:12,292 --> 00:03:13,992
Nothing to spoil your
mom's party, okay?

41
00:03:14,061 --> 00:03:15,661
What, me?

42
00:03:15,729 --> 00:03:17,729
Nah, I wouldn't do
anything like that.

43
00:03:20,167 --> 00:03:22,078
You really think you
got it made, don't you?

44
00:03:22,102 --> 00:03:25,203
Just walking around here
like you own the place.

45
00:03:25,272 --> 00:03:27,439
You don't.

46
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:29,040
My mother does.

47
00:03:29,109 --> 00:03:30,742
And you're just using her.

48
00:03:33,112 --> 00:03:37,048
Let's get something straight
right now, Tony, okay?

49
00:03:37,116 --> 00:03:38,282
I love your mother.

50
00:03:38,352 --> 00:03:39,495
For her money.

51
00:03:39,519 --> 00:03:41,152
And I wouldn't do
anything to hurt her,

52
00:03:41,221 --> 00:03:43,855
which is why I have not told
her what I know about you.

53
00:03:43,923 --> 00:03:46,290
Oh, and what is that, Cal?

54
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:48,560
I was wondering
where you two were.

55
00:03:48,628 --> 00:03:50,668
Mom, hey! I was just
coming to find you.

56
00:03:50,731 --> 00:03:52,174
I thought maybe we'd
have a little dance.

57
00:03:52,198 --> 00:03:53,478
Oh, great.

58
00:03:53,533 --> 00:03:54,899
Excuse me, Tony.

59
00:03:56,303 --> 00:03:57,303
Bonnie,

60
00:03:57,338 --> 00:03:59,170
I believe that's our song.

61
00:03:59,239 --> 00:04:00,739
Next dance, sweetheart?

62
00:04:00,808 --> 00:04:02,240
Yeah, sure.

63
00:04:03,476 --> 00:04:04,576
Shall we?

64
00:04:21,328 --> 00:04:25,530
Oh, that was the nicest birthday
I ever had, Cal, thank you.

65
00:04:26,833 --> 00:04:28,767
My pleasure, love.

66
00:04:31,338 --> 00:04:33,838
Tell Tony the rental
company is coming

67
00:04:33,907 --> 00:04:36,308
to pick up the tables
and chairs at noon today.

68
00:04:36,376 --> 00:04:38,944
Tony was going to go to the
club for a swimming workout.

69
00:04:39,012 --> 00:04:40,479
Can't he go later?

70
00:04:40,547 --> 00:04:42,179
Oh, I'd rather not ask him.

71
00:04:42,249 --> 00:04:43,414
I'll ask him.

72
00:04:43,483 --> 00:04:44,715
You'll order him.

73
00:04:44,784 --> 00:04:46,562
And then he'll get
mad and you'll argue.

74
00:04:46,586 --> 00:04:48,331
And he'll twist you
around his little finger.

75
00:04:48,355 --> 00:04:49,688
He loves me.

76
00:04:49,756 --> 00:04:52,724
When his father died, he
took care of everything, of me.

77
00:04:52,793 --> 00:04:53,959
He needs discipline.

78
00:04:54,027 --> 00:04:57,629
Honey, I want you to
reconsider military school.

79
00:04:57,698 --> 00:04:58,897
No.

80
00:04:58,966 --> 00:05:00,576
Absolutely not. You must.

81
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:01,944
For our sake.

82
00:05:01,968 --> 00:05:03,268
For our sake, Bonnie.

83
00:05:04,471 --> 00:05:06,637
Don't you see it's
happening again?

84
00:05:08,475 --> 00:05:12,277
We argue about
only one thing... Tony.

85
00:05:12,346 --> 00:05:14,279
Always Tony.

86
00:05:16,083 --> 00:05:18,750
Don't you see, Bonnie,
he's tearing us apart?

87
00:05:20,254 --> 00:05:22,087
I love you and I
think you love me.

88
00:05:22,155 --> 00:05:24,756
I do. I want us
to be happy, Cal.

89
00:05:26,559 --> 00:05:28,493
I'll think about it.

90
00:05:28,561 --> 00:05:30,495
Good.

91
00:05:35,168 --> 00:05:37,102
I gotta go. Okay.

92
00:06:03,463 --> 00:06:06,097
Mom! I thought you'd left.

93
00:06:06,166 --> 00:06:09,200
No, Cal did, but I'm waiting
for the rental company.

94
00:06:09,269 --> 00:06:11,469
Could've asked me.

95
00:06:11,538 --> 00:06:13,638
Well, Cal and I knew you
wanted to go swimming.

96
00:06:13,707 --> 00:06:16,408
I wouldn't have minded.

97
00:06:16,476 --> 00:06:17,975
I really am trying, you know.

98
00:06:18,044 --> 00:06:19,276
I know you are,

99
00:06:19,346 --> 00:06:21,679
sweetheart, and I'm
sorry it's been so difficult.

100
00:06:22,983 --> 00:06:25,516
It's just you're here
and Cal's there...

101
00:06:25,585 --> 00:06:27,518
and I'm caught in the middle.

102
00:06:28,722 --> 00:06:31,323
Look, about this
family counseling.

103
00:06:31,391 --> 00:06:33,058
You said you wouldn't go.

104
00:06:33,127 --> 00:06:35,671
Well, I know, but
I've been thinking

105
00:06:35,695 --> 00:06:38,040
and I just want
everything to be okay.

106
00:06:38,064 --> 00:06:41,433
And maybe if Cal and I
spent more time together,

107
00:06:41,502 --> 00:06:43,535
things would work out.

108
00:06:43,604 --> 00:06:45,203
Thank you, sweetie. Thanks.

109
00:07:26,146 --> 00:07:28,079
Hi. Hi.

110
00:07:28,148 --> 00:07:29,214
How are you?

111
00:07:29,283 --> 00:07:31,917
This is a surprise.

112
00:07:31,985 --> 00:07:33,518
We need to talk.

113
00:07:33,586 --> 00:07:35,187
Sure.

114
00:07:35,255 --> 00:07:36,621
Okay, just in there?

115
00:07:36,690 --> 00:07:37,956
Sure, you bet.

116
00:07:40,828 --> 00:07:43,761
I'm sorry about this morning.

117
00:07:43,831 --> 00:07:46,391
Tony says that he's
ready for family counseling.

118
00:07:47,434 --> 00:07:49,000
Yeah? Yeah.

119
00:07:49,069 --> 00:07:53,638
Uh, well, uh, Tony
has said that before.

120
00:07:53,707 --> 00:07:54,873
And if you remember, we went.

121
00:07:54,942 --> 00:07:56,374
He never bothered to show up.

122
00:07:56,443 --> 00:07:59,610
I know, but this time, he
says that he wants to go.

123
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:02,692
He needs to know
that we trust him.

124
00:08:02,716 --> 00:08:04,716
I don't anymore.

125
00:08:04,784 --> 00:08:07,352
So what, we just
make him disappear?

126
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:09,299
We just ship him off
to school, is that it?

127
00:08:09,323 --> 00:08:11,756
If you want to save
this marriage, and I do.

128
00:08:11,825 --> 00:08:14,225
We've been through
this so many times before.

129
00:08:14,294 --> 00:08:15,571
When is it going to be resolved?

130
00:08:15,595 --> 00:08:17,061
Bonnie, listen to me.

131
00:08:17,130 --> 00:08:18,730
Our relationship can't last

132
00:08:18,799 --> 00:08:21,232
with the three of us
in the same household.

133
00:08:21,301 --> 00:08:23,234
Open your eyes.

134
00:08:23,303 --> 00:08:26,404
It's more than a
personality conflict.

135
00:08:26,473 --> 00:08:28,306
Tony lies.

136
00:08:28,375 --> 00:08:29,686
Tony cheats. How dare you!

137
00:08:29,710 --> 00:08:31,108
Tony's my son.

138
00:08:31,177 --> 00:08:33,010
I'm not going to send him away.

139
00:08:33,079 --> 00:08:34,912
You can't handle that...

140
00:08:34,981 --> 00:08:36,314
then you can leave.

141
00:08:37,417 --> 00:08:38,783
That's no solution.

142
00:08:38,852 --> 00:08:42,187
I'll do whatever I have
to, to protect Tony.

143
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:44,955
Even from you.

144
00:08:48,028 --> 00:08:49,238
Mr. Ritter, I'm
sorry Mrs. Ritter.

145
00:08:49,262 --> 00:08:50,361
It's 5:00

146
00:08:50,430 --> 00:08:52,430
and my ride's here. I
just wanted to let you

147
00:08:52,499 --> 00:08:54,665
know I was leaving.

148
00:08:54,734 --> 00:08:56,301
I'll see you.

149
00:09:12,118 --> 00:09:14,363
Here she is, Mr. Morgen,
all washed and dry.

150
00:09:14,387 --> 00:09:16,321
Thanks.

151
00:09:17,824 --> 00:09:19,035
You have a good day, sir.

152
00:09:19,059 --> 00:09:20,539
Uh, Mario. Hey. Yes, sir?

153
00:09:23,296 --> 00:09:24,440
Thank you, sir.

154
00:09:24,464 --> 00:09:25,744
You drive carefully.

155
00:09:48,821 --> 00:09:51,033
I'm sorry to call you
so late in the day,

156
00:09:51,057 --> 00:09:53,236
but I need to cancel our
meeting tomorrow morning.

157
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:55,894
I hope this isn't too much
of an inconvenience for you,

158
00:09:55,962 --> 00:09:58,341
but I've got a big deal
going with some clients

159
00:09:58,365 --> 00:10:00,009
from the West Coast... Hello?

160
00:10:00,033 --> 00:10:02,299
Hold on. I'm here.

161
00:10:02,368 --> 00:10:03,635
Hello?

162
00:10:03,703 --> 00:10:05,670
Hi, Cal, it's Dan.
Yeah, hi, Dan.

163
00:10:05,739 --> 00:10:07,149
How're you doing?
I've got to reschedule.

164
00:10:07,173 --> 00:10:08,885
Is Wednesday okay? Uh-huh.

165
00:10:08,909 --> 00:10:10,408
No, no, Wednesday will be fine.

166
00:10:10,476 --> 00:10:11,676
I've got to go out of town.

167
00:10:11,745 --> 00:10:13,011
Yeah, have a great trip.

168
00:10:13,079 --> 00:10:14,919
Thanks. See you then. Bye-bye.

169
00:10:22,089 --> 00:10:23,688
Hi.

170
00:10:23,757 --> 00:10:24,856
Thought I'd drop by.

171
00:10:27,427 --> 00:10:29,627
Table looks good.

172
00:10:30,763 --> 00:10:32,630
What is that? Uh...

173
00:10:32,698 --> 00:10:34,632
Is that oak?

174
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:36,801
Very nice. I like that.

175
00:10:36,869 --> 00:10:38,836
I like the feel of wood.

176
00:10:38,905 --> 00:10:40,838
Good.

177
00:10:42,208 --> 00:10:44,075
You've got a lot of
nice things here, Cal.

178
00:10:44,144 --> 00:10:46,410
I should probably
visit you more often.

179
00:10:48,214 --> 00:10:50,915
I think it would be interesting
to learn about your business.

180
00:10:50,984 --> 00:10:52,317
Maybe we could work out

181
00:10:52,385 --> 00:10:55,019
some kind of a
part-time kind of thing?

182
00:10:55,088 --> 00:10:58,089
Of course, I'd, I'd have to
commute from military school,

183
00:10:58,158 --> 00:11:01,692
so I-I probably couldn't,
couldn't come every day.

184
00:11:04,097 --> 00:11:06,264
You're not sending
me to military school.

185
00:11:06,333 --> 00:11:08,465
Oh, yes, I am.

186
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:13,004
No, no, I'm not going anywhere.

187
00:11:13,073 --> 00:11:15,051
Well, you don't have
any choice in the matter,

188
00:11:15,075 --> 00:11:16,707
Tony.

189
00:11:16,776 --> 00:11:18,443
Either you go voluntarily,

190
00:11:18,511 --> 00:11:19,744
or I tell your mother

191
00:11:19,813 --> 00:11:22,680
what I know. Yeah,
and what is that, Cal?

192
00:11:22,749 --> 00:11:25,116
For one thing, I should
never have fired Shirley.

193
00:11:30,022 --> 00:11:31,534
You stole this,
didn't you, Tony?

194
00:11:31,558 --> 00:11:33,290
And you stole everything else

195
00:11:33,359 --> 00:11:35,459
that I accused
Shirley of stealing.

196
00:11:37,030 --> 00:11:38,641
What did you, uh,
tell Mom about that?

197
00:11:38,665 --> 00:11:41,466
Well, I told her I misplaced
it and I just found it again.

198
00:11:41,535 --> 00:11:43,134
But if you push me,

199
00:11:43,203 --> 00:11:44,735
I'll tell her that you stole it.

200
00:11:46,039 --> 00:11:47,505
The point is, Tony,

201
00:11:47,574 --> 00:11:49,340
pawn shop records
have your name on them.

202
00:11:49,409 --> 00:11:52,977
And there is nothing that
you can do to change that.

203
00:11:53,046 --> 00:11:54,879
Case closed.

204
00:12:05,859 --> 00:12:07,758
You're wrong, Cal.

205
00:12:44,564 --> 00:12:46,497
♪ ♪

206
00:13:17,329 --> 00:13:18,473
Yes?

207
00:13:18,497 --> 00:13:20,731
Are you Mrs. Calvin Ritter? Yes.

208
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:23,133
I'm Lieutenant Bob
Brooks, Atlanta Police.

209
00:13:23,203 --> 00:13:26,904
There's been a fire at
your husband's studio.

210
00:13:26,973 --> 00:13:28,540
Oh, my God.

211
00:13:28,608 --> 00:13:29,874
Is Cal okay?

212
00:13:29,943 --> 00:13:31,475
I'm sorry.

213
00:13:31,544 --> 00:13:32,843
Fire spread fast.

214
00:13:32,913 --> 00:13:33,978
Your husband was dead

215
00:13:34,047 --> 00:13:35,179
when we got there.

216
00:13:35,248 --> 00:13:37,248
Oh, Cal.

217
00:13:37,317 --> 00:13:38,949
Cal.

218
00:13:42,188 --> 00:13:43,921
Oh, Cal.

219
00:13:46,459 --> 00:13:48,125
Tony.

220
00:13:49,562 --> 00:13:50,961
Tony!

221
00:13:51,030 --> 00:13:52,196
Mom, what's going on?

222
00:13:52,265 --> 00:13:53,698
What happened?

223
00:13:54,801 --> 00:13:56,301
It's Cal.

224
00:13:56,369 --> 00:13:57,568
He's dead.

225
00:13:57,637 --> 00:13:58,836
How?

226
00:13:58,905 --> 00:14:00,616
There was a fire in his studio.

227
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:04,008
Lots of flammable
material around.

228
00:14:04,077 --> 00:14:05,810
He was always so careful.

229
00:14:05,879 --> 00:14:08,439
Yes, ma'am, I'm...
I'm sure he was.

230
00:14:09,115 --> 00:14:11,749
We have a reason to suspect
arson and a possible homicide.

231
00:14:12,985 --> 00:14:14,563
I'm, uh, sorry, Mrs. Ritter.

232
00:14:14,587 --> 00:14:16,432
Uh, I know this is
not the best time,

233
00:14:16,456 --> 00:14:18,022
but I'm going to have to ask you

234
00:14:18,091 --> 00:14:19,702
to accompany us
downtown to the station.

235
00:14:19,726 --> 00:14:21,704
Now?! I'm sorry,

236
00:14:21,728 --> 00:14:23,840
but there's lots of questions
that need answering.

237
00:14:23,864 --> 00:14:26,175
All right, let me go
with you. No, no, no, no.

238
00:14:26,199 --> 00:14:27,399
You should stay.

239
00:14:27,467 --> 00:14:29,700
Stay here and...
and call... call Evie,

240
00:14:29,769 --> 00:14:31,936
and I'll... I'll take care of...

241
00:14:32,005 --> 00:14:33,349
things. All right. Okay.

242
00:14:33,373 --> 00:14:34,583
All right.

243
00:14:34,607 --> 00:14:36,174
I'll take care of everything.

244
00:14:36,243 --> 00:14:37,808
I promise.

245
00:14:42,649 --> 00:14:44,715
Ben, I don't understand
what's going on.

246
00:14:44,784 --> 00:14:46,150
Why am I here?

247
00:14:46,218 --> 00:14:48,886
They think that Cal was killed.

248
00:14:54,227 --> 00:14:57,327
Bonnie, they think it was you.

249
00:14:59,032 --> 00:15:00,664
Me? Why?

250
00:15:00,734 --> 00:15:02,633
What time did you leave Cal?

251
00:15:03,737 --> 00:15:06,137
Uh... I don't... I don't know.

252
00:15:06,206 --> 00:15:08,906
Um... ten after 5:00?

253
00:15:08,975 --> 00:15:11,041
Couldn't have been
earlier than that?

254
00:15:11,111 --> 00:15:13,377
No. Um, the guard came in

255
00:15:13,446 --> 00:15:16,214
to say he was going,
and that was at 5:00.

256
00:15:16,282 --> 00:15:18,449
And I-I left a few
minutes after that.

257
00:15:23,389 --> 00:15:28,292
The clocks in Cal's
studio stopped at 5:15.

258
00:15:28,361 --> 00:15:30,961
According to the fire marshal,

259
00:15:31,030 --> 00:15:34,165
that means you were
there when the fire started.

260
00:15:43,709 --> 00:15:45,676
I'm, uh, sorry to
have to do this,

261
00:15:45,745 --> 00:15:47,123
but in light of the evidence...

262
00:15:47,147 --> 00:15:49,057
Your presence at the scene

263
00:15:49,081 --> 00:15:50,760
moments prior to the crime,

264
00:15:50,784 --> 00:15:51,983
your lack of an alibi

265
00:15:52,051 --> 00:15:53,684
at the estimated
time of the fire

266
00:15:53,753 --> 00:15:56,920
and eyewitness testimony...

267
00:15:56,989 --> 00:16:00,124
I'm sorry, I have no choice
but to place you under arrest

268
00:16:00,193 --> 00:16:02,893
for the charge of
murder in the first degree.

269
00:16:12,472 --> 00:16:14,238
Oh.

270
00:16:14,307 --> 00:16:16,407
Arson, huh?

271
00:16:16,476 --> 00:16:18,643
No faulty wiring.

272
00:16:18,711 --> 00:16:20,911
Everything seemed
to be in order.

273
00:16:22,348 --> 00:16:23,492
Yo, Ben.

274
00:16:23,516 --> 00:16:25,115
Hi, Bob.

275
00:16:27,019 --> 00:16:28,299
Glad I caught you.

276
00:16:28,354 --> 00:16:29,487
What's up?

277
00:16:29,556 --> 00:16:31,855
Well...

278
00:16:31,924 --> 00:16:33,324
it's real embarrassing.

279
00:16:33,393 --> 00:16:35,426
Uh... boys down at the station

280
00:16:35,495 --> 00:16:37,695
were cataloging items
that we took from the scene

281
00:16:37,731 --> 00:16:38,941
of the crime.

282
00:16:38,965 --> 00:16:41,377
There was a tape and a
phone answering machine.

283
00:16:41,401 --> 00:16:43,111
We played it down,
and there it was.

284
00:16:43,135 --> 00:16:44,380
Cal Ritter must have picked up

285
00:16:44,404 --> 00:16:45,781
in the middle of his message

286
00:16:45,805 --> 00:16:49,707
'cause his voice is there
answering a call at 5:25,

287
00:16:49,776 --> 00:16:52,087
which means someone
set back the clocks.

288
00:16:52,111 --> 00:16:55,246
Well, if my client's got
an alibi for that time,

289
00:16:55,314 --> 00:16:57,354
you might as well
start dropping charges.

290
00:16:57,417 --> 00:16:58,761
Yeah.

291
00:16:58,785 --> 00:17:00,751
You know, uh,

292
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:01,985
wouldn't hurt to...

293
00:17:02,054 --> 00:17:03,487
wash your car once a year.

294
00:17:03,556 --> 00:17:05,289
I did.

295
00:17:05,358 --> 00:17:06,857
March 31.

296
00:17:06,926 --> 00:17:09,046
Huh.

297
00:17:09,095 --> 00:17:10,439
Hey.

298
00:17:10,463 --> 00:17:12,383
There's a lot of
that going around.

299
00:17:13,433 --> 00:17:15,265
Damn.

300
00:17:16,536 --> 00:17:18,013
Now, let's see.

301
00:17:18,037 --> 00:17:22,372
The electricity went
off at, uh... uh, 5:15,

302
00:17:22,442 --> 00:17:26,076
and-and that's when the police
thought the murder took place,

303
00:17:26,145 --> 00:17:29,847
but Cal answered a call at 5:25,

304
00:17:29,916 --> 00:17:33,550
so all we have to know
is where you were at 5:25.

305
00:17:33,620 --> 00:17:35,786
In the car.

306
00:17:35,855 --> 00:17:38,889
Well, did you make a
call or stop at the house?

307
00:17:38,958 --> 00:17:40,724
Uh-uh.

308
00:17:40,793 --> 00:17:41,992
Got gas?

309
00:17:42,061 --> 00:17:43,360
No.

310
00:17:43,430 --> 00:17:45,763
Groceries, ice cream cone?

311
00:17:46,866 --> 00:17:49,199
No, I-I just drove around.

312
00:17:49,268 --> 00:17:51,402
I needed to think.

313
00:17:51,471 --> 00:17:53,804
I'm sorry. I didn't go anywhere.

314
00:17:53,873 --> 00:17:55,806
I didn't talk to anyone.

315
00:17:56,910 --> 00:17:59,110
Well, the police will
only have your word

316
00:17:59,178 --> 00:18:02,113
that you actually left
Cal's shop at 5:10.

317
00:18:02,182 --> 00:18:03,514
I'm sorry.

318
00:18:03,583 --> 00:18:05,483
I know you are.

319
00:18:05,552 --> 00:18:07,651
I am, too.

320
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:09,520
It was worth a shot.

321
00:18:09,589 --> 00:18:10,721
Ice tea?

322
00:18:13,193 --> 00:18:14,736
Uh, Miss Malone, here let me...

323
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:16,738
Oh, thank you. Let
me help you with that.

324
00:18:16,762 --> 00:18:18,541
It's very heavy.
Oh, it is, isn't it?

325
00:18:18,565 --> 00:18:19,664
Thank you.

326
00:18:19,732 --> 00:18:20,976
Where's-Where's the maid?

327
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,768
Oh, Cal, uh, fired her about
two weeks ago for stealing.

328
00:18:24,837 --> 00:18:26,671
Can you imagine,
after eight years?

329
00:18:26,739 --> 00:18:27,916
Shirley was stealing?

330
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:29,660
Well, Cal wouldn't
have fired her otherwise.

331
00:18:29,709 --> 00:18:31,709
I mean, he was the nicest man.

332
00:18:31,777 --> 00:18:33,211
Huh.

333
00:18:33,279 --> 00:18:36,180
You know, I just don't know
what she's gonna do without him.

334
00:18:36,249 --> 00:18:38,329
Huh.

335
00:18:40,920 --> 00:18:42,085
Excuse me.

336
00:18:42,154 --> 00:18:43,487
Yeah.

337
00:18:43,556 --> 00:18:46,257
Hmm. Here.

338
00:18:50,997 --> 00:18:52,475
Hi, everybody. Oh, hi, Tony.

339
00:18:52,499 --> 00:18:54,409
Hi, Mr. Matlock.
Nice to see you again.

340
00:18:54,433 --> 00:18:55,945
Nice to see you.
Uh, Miss Thomas?

341
00:18:55,969 --> 00:18:57,368
Hi. I'm Tony Morgan. Oh.

342
00:18:57,436 --> 00:18:59,904
I just want to thank you for
all you're doing for my mother.

343
00:18:59,973 --> 00:19:01,049
Sure. How are you, Mom?

344
00:19:01,073 --> 00:19:02,173
Hi.

345
00:19:02,242 --> 00:19:05,209
It had to be a robbery, Ben.

346
00:19:05,278 --> 00:19:06,722
It's the only thing
that makes sense.

347
00:19:06,746 --> 00:19:09,113
Well, there was no
sign of forced entry.

348
00:19:09,181 --> 00:19:11,815
Did Cal seem worried

349
00:19:11,885 --> 00:19:13,028
or nervous lately?

350
00:19:13,052 --> 00:19:14,897
No, no. He was in a great mood.

351
00:19:14,921 --> 00:19:17,922
He threw a huge party for Mom
here at the house on Saturday.

352
00:19:17,991 --> 00:19:19,357
No.

353
00:19:19,425 --> 00:19:21,225
Uh, listen, Mr. Matlock,
uh, I don't want

354
00:19:21,294 --> 00:19:23,727
to sound rude or anything,
but, uh, my mom's exhausted,

355
00:19:23,796 --> 00:19:25,196
and she's been through a lot.

356
00:19:25,265 --> 00:19:26,663
And I'm wondering
if we can maybe

357
00:19:26,732 --> 00:19:28,143
just do this another
day, another time.

358
00:19:28,167 --> 00:19:29,544
Oh, oh, certainly,
certainly, certainly.

359
00:19:29,568 --> 00:19:30,934
Bonnie, we'll, uh...

360
00:19:31,004 --> 00:19:34,204
we'll see you later on. Okay.

361
00:19:36,276 --> 00:19:37,642
I don't know, Ben.

362
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:39,543
I don't, either.

363
00:19:39,612 --> 00:19:41,545
Cal Ritter didn't
have any enemies.

364
00:19:41,614 --> 00:19:43,547
He had one.

365
00:19:52,124 --> 00:19:54,057
Shirley?!

366
00:19:54,127 --> 00:19:58,262
Mr. Matlock.

367
00:19:58,331 --> 00:19:59,997
How are you?

368
00:20:00,066 --> 00:20:01,331
Just fine.

369
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:03,746
Uh, I guess you haven't heard.

370
00:20:03,770 --> 00:20:05,981
I'm defending Bonnie Ritter.

371
00:20:06,005 --> 00:20:08,305
I heard. Poor thing.

372
00:20:08,374 --> 00:20:10,686
Maybe you could help
me with something.

373
00:20:10,710 --> 00:20:12,809
I'm late for work.

374
00:20:12,879 --> 00:20:14,278
Get in. I'll give you a lift.

375
00:20:14,347 --> 00:20:16,467
Thank you.

376
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:25,122
So, you worked for, uh,
Mrs. Ritter for how long?

377
00:20:25,191 --> 00:20:26,857
Would have been
eight years next month.

378
00:20:26,926 --> 00:20:28,625
Oh, a member of the family.

379
00:20:28,694 --> 00:20:30,794
My family's at home.

380
00:20:30,863 --> 00:20:33,497
<i>Mr. Matlock. Hi.</i>

381
00:20:33,566 --> 00:20:35,511
How you doing? Okay.

382
00:20:35,535 --> 00:20:36,767
My mom's not here.

383
00:20:36,836 --> 00:20:38,202
I know.

384
00:20:38,270 --> 00:20:39,550
Go ahead with what you're doing.

385
00:20:39,606 --> 00:20:41,906
Yeah, I was, I was
just helping my mother

386
00:20:41,975 --> 00:20:43,095
go through Cal's papers.

387
00:20:43,142 --> 00:20:44,587
You know, he
wasn't very organized.

388
00:20:44,611 --> 00:20:47,645
Oh, that's a big responsibility.

389
00:20:47,714 --> 00:20:49,646
Yeah.

390
00:20:49,716 --> 00:20:51,548
Uh, when my father
died, I was only 13.

391
00:20:51,617 --> 00:20:54,317
Um, but Mom and I did just fine.

392
00:20:56,055 --> 00:20:59,523
It must've been very difficult
for you when she remarried.

393
00:20:59,592 --> 00:21:02,025
I was happy for
her, Mr. Matlock.

394
00:21:02,095 --> 00:21:04,295
I mean, uh... Cal
was a great guy.

395
00:21:04,363 --> 00:21:05,563
And, uh, he made Mom happy

396
00:21:05,631 --> 00:21:06,775
and that's really
all that matters.

397
00:21:06,799 --> 00:21:10,000
Yeah. So you and
Cal got along okay?

398
00:21:10,069 --> 00:21:11,702
Oh, yeah.

399
00:21:11,771 --> 00:21:13,082
I mean, we
understood each other.

400
00:21:13,106 --> 00:21:16,140
You never, you
never fought with Cal

401
00:21:16,209 --> 00:21:18,075
or spied on him and your mother?

402
00:21:18,144 --> 00:21:19,243
"Spied"?

403
00:21:22,581 --> 00:21:24,615
Where... where did
you get that from?

404
00:21:24,683 --> 00:21:27,484
From your housekeeper... former.

405
00:21:27,553 --> 00:21:28,919
Shirley Taylor.

406
00:21:28,988 --> 00:21:30,955
Shirley Taylor.

407
00:21:31,023 --> 00:21:33,023
I'll tell ya, had Mom and
me completely fooled.

408
00:21:33,092 --> 00:21:36,326
As a matter of fact, it
took Cal to figure her out.

409
00:21:36,396 --> 00:21:39,597
You know what a
kleptomaniac is, Mr. Matlock?

410
00:21:39,665 --> 00:21:41,298
Yeah.

411
00:21:44,270 --> 00:21:45,569
I used to know a fella

412
00:21:45,638 --> 00:21:47,916
that pronounced that
word "kleptominerac."

413
00:21:49,909 --> 00:21:53,210
So, uh, Shirley was
a compulsive thief?

414
00:21:53,279 --> 00:21:54,779
Yeah. I know.

415
00:21:54,847 --> 00:21:56,680
I mean, she seemed
like such a nice person.

416
00:21:56,749 --> 00:21:59,616
But, um, Cal mentioned
that her lying and her stealing

417
00:21:59,685 --> 00:22:03,053
was just a sickness, so... Ah.

418
00:22:03,122 --> 00:22:04,521
Did you go to her home?

419
00:22:04,590 --> 00:22:07,624
Uh, no. No, I was,
uh, on my way there

420
00:22:07,693 --> 00:22:10,360
and I saw her walking along
the, the street going to work.

421
00:22:10,429 --> 00:22:13,163
So I picked her up
and gave her a lift... car.

422
00:22:13,232 --> 00:22:15,376
So, anyway, tell your
mother I stopped by.

423
00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:17,960
Okay, well, let me
walk you out. Okay.

424
00:22:23,509 --> 00:22:25,443
Boy!

425
00:22:25,511 --> 00:22:27,711
That's a beautiful car.

426
00:22:27,780 --> 00:22:29,060
The man had good taste.

427
00:22:29,115 --> 00:22:30,381
Yeah, he did.

428
00:22:30,450 --> 00:22:32,049
Cal loved this car.

429
00:22:32,118 --> 00:22:36,587
Um, I just, uh, I
can't remember.

430
00:22:36,655 --> 00:22:40,758
Uh, the day Cal died,
uh, where were you?

431
00:22:40,827 --> 00:22:42,759
At the club.

432
00:22:42,829 --> 00:22:45,229
And you left about, um...?

433
00:22:45,298 --> 00:22:46,896
Little after 5:00.

434
00:22:46,966 --> 00:22:51,335
So that would, that'd put you home, what?
- 30.

435
00:22:51,403 --> 00:22:53,136
Yeah. Uh-huh.

436
00:22:53,205 --> 00:22:55,072
Was anybody in
the house but you?

437
00:22:55,140 --> 00:22:57,140
No, I was alone.

438
00:22:57,209 --> 00:22:59,142
Oh.

439
00:23:08,420 --> 00:23:12,322
Huh? You better have
your car washed, Tony.

440
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:16,060
That could cause
trouble later on.

441
00:23:37,049 --> 00:23:41,452
You know, every time I saw
Cal, he was wearing this jacket.

442
00:23:41,520 --> 00:23:43,532
I had to talk Cal
into buying it.

443
00:23:43,556 --> 00:23:46,690
He said it was too expensive.

444
00:23:46,759 --> 00:23:49,271
Sometimes people that
are born and raised poor,

445
00:23:49,295 --> 00:23:51,855
they have trouble spending
money on themselves.

446
00:23:53,065 --> 00:23:54,999
I told him he deserved it.

447
00:23:56,702 --> 00:23:57,802
Where's the lighter?

448
00:23:57,870 --> 00:24:00,637
"The lighter"? Yeah.

449
00:24:01,774 --> 00:24:02,873
Was it valuable?

450
00:24:02,942 --> 00:24:06,444
Antique, 18 karat gold.

451
00:24:06,512 --> 00:24:09,045
It had the initial "B"
in diamonds on it.

452
00:24:09,115 --> 00:24:11,715
Ben, I'm telling you,

453
00:24:11,783 --> 00:24:13,584
somebody broke in there,

454
00:24:13,652 --> 00:24:16,212
and they robbed Cal
and they killed him.

455
00:24:18,491 --> 00:24:25,962
Well, there's the
remains of Cal's watch.

456
00:24:26,031 --> 00:24:29,144
That's an odd business that
somebody would take that lighter

457
00:24:29,168 --> 00:24:33,270
and leave this expensive
gold watch behind.

458
00:24:36,275 --> 00:24:38,342
When I think of some
of the things I said,

459
00:24:38,410 --> 00:24:40,344
I'll never stop being sorry.

460
00:24:55,027 --> 00:24:56,987
Good afternoon, ma'am. Hi.

461
00:24:58,931 --> 00:25:00,130
Oh, I'm not staying.

462
00:25:00,198 --> 00:25:01,899
Actually, I need to talk

463
00:25:01,967 --> 00:25:04,067
to whoever was
working around 5:00

464
00:25:04,136 --> 00:25:05,535
on Sunday the 15th.

465
00:25:05,604 --> 00:25:07,004
Oh, not me, that's for sure.

466
00:25:07,072 --> 00:25:08,182
Sunday's I kick back.

467
00:25:08,206 --> 00:25:09,451
Oh. That'd be Mario and Eric.

468
00:25:09,475 --> 00:25:11,275
Okay, either one of
them around now?

469
00:25:11,344 --> 00:25:12,624
Oh, they're due in an hour.

470
00:25:12,678 --> 00:25:15,145
Hmm...

471
00:25:15,214 --> 00:25:16,413
Well, I'll come back.

472
00:25:16,482 --> 00:25:19,049
Thanks.

473
00:25:19,117 --> 00:25:20,917
Have a good one. Thank you.

474
00:25:23,155 --> 00:25:26,756
<i>There was no
sign of forced entry.</i>

475
00:25:26,826 --> 00:25:30,593
And in your experience,
what does that mean?

476
00:25:30,662 --> 00:25:33,496
Well, whoever entered
was known by the victim.

477
00:25:34,667 --> 00:25:36,900
The fire started
sometime after 5:15.

478
00:25:36,969 --> 00:25:39,736
And given the
answering machine tape,

479
00:25:39,805 --> 00:25:41,404
probably about 5:30.

480
00:25:41,474 --> 00:25:43,406
Thank you, Captain.

481
00:25:45,043 --> 00:25:47,310
Mrs. Ritter was still
there when I left at 5:00.

482
00:25:47,379 --> 00:25:49,612
She told Mr. Ritter
to pack up and leave.

483
00:25:49,681 --> 00:25:52,049
I've never seen a woman so mad.

484
00:25:52,118 --> 00:25:54,284
Thank you. No further questions.

485
00:25:55,521 --> 00:25:56,620
Cross-examine?

486
00:25:56,689 --> 00:25:59,622
No questions at this
time, Your Honor.

487
00:26:01,126 --> 00:26:03,827
Due to the hour, this
court will stand in recess

488
00:26:03,895 --> 00:26:05,962
until 9:00 a.m.
tomorrow morning.

489
00:26:17,977 --> 00:26:20,744
Didn't expect all that.

490
00:26:20,813 --> 00:26:22,558
Well, we didn't even start yet.

491
00:26:22,582 --> 00:26:25,126
I'm not a bit worried, no.

492
00:26:25,150 --> 00:26:26,261
Don't you be, okay?

493
00:26:26,285 --> 00:26:28,318
Mm-hmm. What you
need is to go home

494
00:26:28,387 --> 00:26:30,320
and get some rest. Right.

495
00:26:31,991 --> 00:26:34,324
Thanks, Mr. Matlock. Yeah.

496
00:26:34,393 --> 00:26:35,492
Oh, Michelle?

497
00:26:37,362 --> 00:26:40,830
You think you could
see, uh, Bonnie, home?

498
00:26:40,899 --> 00:26:42,966
There are a couple
things maybe Tony could

499
00:26:43,034 --> 00:26:45,435
help me out with. Fine.

500
00:26:46,305 --> 00:26:47,537
Okay with you?

501
00:26:47,606 --> 00:26:48,750
Yeah, sure. Okay.

502
00:26:48,774 --> 00:26:49,873
Now, remember.

503
00:26:49,942 --> 00:26:51,507
I do the worrying.

504
00:26:51,577 --> 00:26:52,642
Reassure her.

505
00:27:00,987 --> 00:27:02,886
Well...

506
00:27:05,657 --> 00:27:07,802
Well, uh, I'll be
honest with you,

507
00:27:07,826 --> 00:27:10,394
it's not really going very well.

508
00:27:10,463 --> 00:27:12,329
No?

509
00:27:12,398 --> 00:27:15,577
No, uh... I mean, I appreciate
you not wanting Mom to worry,

510
00:27:15,601 --> 00:27:17,512
but you have absolutely nothing

511
00:27:17,536 --> 00:27:20,337
to prove her innocence.

512
00:27:20,406 --> 00:27:23,140
Well, that, that may be true.

513
00:27:23,209 --> 00:27:26,643
And it's interesting
that you think that.

514
00:27:28,047 --> 00:27:32,082
I'm not saying that
the D.A. isn't, uh,

515
00:27:32,151 --> 00:27:33,583
doing the best she can.

516
00:27:33,652 --> 00:27:37,321
And she does have a
few credible witnesses.

517
00:27:37,390 --> 00:27:40,491
B-But look at all the
things she doesn't have.

518
00:27:40,559 --> 00:27:44,294
There's no, no blood stains,

519
00:27:44,363 --> 00:27:46,063
no fingerprints.

520
00:27:46,132 --> 00:27:47,797
There's no physical evidence.

521
00:27:47,866 --> 00:27:50,901
There's not one thing
to directly connect

522
00:27:50,969 --> 00:27:53,970
your mother with
the fire or the murder.

523
00:27:54,040 --> 00:27:55,805
That's a big problem. Hmm.

524
00:27:55,874 --> 00:27:57,874
Juries like things obvious.

525
00:27:57,943 --> 00:27:59,321
So you think Mom will get off?

526
00:27:59,345 --> 00:28:00,977
Oh, yeah, she didn't kill Cal.

527
00:28:01,047 --> 00:28:02,224
That's great. Yeah.

528
00:28:02,248 --> 00:28:03,392
All right, see you tomorrow.

529
00:28:03,416 --> 00:28:05,349
Okay.

530
00:28:07,286 --> 00:28:08,818
Hey, Tony?

531
00:28:08,888 --> 00:28:10,165
Tony?

532
00:28:10,189 --> 00:28:12,055
Let, let me...

533
00:28:12,124 --> 00:28:14,524
Uh...

534
00:28:14,593 --> 00:28:17,561
Uh, see, it's not that

535
00:28:17,630 --> 00:28:20,196
nobody cares <i>who</i> killed Cal.

536
00:28:20,266 --> 00:28:22,143
As soon as your
mother's acquitted,

537
00:28:22,167 --> 00:28:23,811
the police will reopen
this investigation.

538
00:28:23,835 --> 00:28:27,170
And there's one thing
about Lieutenant Brooks.

539
00:28:27,239 --> 00:28:29,005
He does not give up.

540
00:28:29,074 --> 00:28:30,285
Okay. Great. Okay?

541
00:28:30,309 --> 00:28:31,519
Bye. All right.

542
00:28:31,543 --> 00:28:33,310
Excuse me.

543
00:28:33,379 --> 00:28:34,978
Oh, hey, Tony! Tony! Yeah?

544
00:28:36,382 --> 00:28:38,693
Uh, I have something to ask you.

545
00:28:38,717 --> 00:28:42,185
Uh, I just... uh,
don't remember.

546
00:28:42,254 --> 00:28:45,589
Um, the day Cal died,

547
00:28:45,658 --> 00:28:48,218
what time did you
say you got home?

548
00:28:48,260 --> 00:28:49,959
Uh, 5:30.

549
00:28:50,028 --> 00:28:51,461
Uh-huh.

550
00:28:51,530 --> 00:28:52,862
Could it have been, uh,

551
00:28:52,931 --> 00:28:55,332
you know, five minutes earlier

552
00:28:55,401 --> 00:28:56,767
or five minutes later?

553
00:28:56,835 --> 00:28:59,503
Uh, yes, yeah.

554
00:28:59,571 --> 00:29:00,971
Uh... No.

555
00:29:01,006 --> 00:29:03,340
It's interesting

556
00:29:03,409 --> 00:29:07,343
how casual people
are, uh, about time.

557
00:29:07,413 --> 00:29:10,580
A person will tell you
that he'll meet you at, uh,

558
00:29:10,649 --> 00:29:14,183
8:00, and he shows up at 8:15.

559
00:29:15,454 --> 00:29:16,731
You know that? Yeah, yeah.

560
00:29:16,755 --> 00:29:19,489
As if, uh, you know, a few
minutes on way or the other

561
00:29:19,558 --> 00:29:20,957
doesn't make any difference.

562
00:29:21,026 --> 00:29:22,992
But sometimes,
that can be important.

563
00:29:23,062 --> 00:29:26,496
Like we all thought

564
00:29:26,565 --> 00:29:29,266
the fire started around 5:00.

565
00:29:29,334 --> 00:29:33,136
'Cause that's what the
killer wanted us to think.

566
00:29:33,205 --> 00:29:35,572
How could he have known

567
00:29:35,640 --> 00:29:38,308
that Cal would receive a
call on his answering machine

568
00:29:38,377 --> 00:29:42,512
and answer it
personally at 5:25?

569
00:29:42,581 --> 00:29:43,992
See?

570
00:29:44,016 --> 00:29:47,551
So now, we believe
the fire started later.

571
00:29:48,887 --> 00:29:52,422
And somebody
fiddled with the clocks.

572
00:29:52,491 --> 00:29:54,958
So the killer, now,

573
00:29:55,027 --> 00:29:57,494
has an alibi for
the supposed time,

574
00:29:57,563 --> 00:29:59,896
but not for the real time.

575
00:29:59,965 --> 00:30:01,108
Hmm. Yeah.

576
00:30:01,132 --> 00:30:02,410
I didn't know that. Yeah.

577
00:30:02,434 --> 00:30:04,345
That's interesting.
Yeah, well, I'll see you.

578
00:30:04,369 --> 00:30:06,249
All right. Okay.

579
00:30:06,705 --> 00:30:08,205
Uh, hey, Tony?

580
00:30:08,273 --> 00:30:10,106
Tony, uh...

581
00:30:10,175 --> 00:30:12,542
Sorry, just one other thing.

582
00:30:12,611 --> 00:30:15,178
Uh, you remember this morning

583
00:30:15,247 --> 00:30:16,713
when the guard was on the stand?

584
00:30:16,781 --> 00:30:19,882
He said that, that Cal had, uh,

585
00:30:19,951 --> 00:30:23,420
well, threatened
to send you away.

586
00:30:23,488 --> 00:30:25,088
Yeah.

587
00:30:25,156 --> 00:30:27,056
Well, clear something up for me.

588
00:30:27,125 --> 00:30:29,992
Uh, I found a pile

589
00:30:30,061 --> 00:30:33,930
of military school
brochures in Cal's office.

590
00:30:35,367 --> 00:30:36,644
Well, there's
nothing to clear up.

591
00:30:36,668 --> 00:30:38,346
It's just that, uh, you
know lots of families

592
00:30:38,370 --> 00:30:39,947
send their kids away
to boarding school.

593
00:30:39,971 --> 00:30:41,683
It's something that
Cal and I talked about.

594
00:30:41,707 --> 00:30:43,240
Yeah, yeah, but here you are,

595
00:30:43,308 --> 00:30:45,675
it's the middle of
your senior year

596
00:30:45,744 --> 00:30:48,911
and almost all the
applications for college are in,

597
00:30:48,980 --> 00:30:50,614
and it just doesn't make sense.

598
00:30:51,916 --> 00:30:53,328
It didn't happen, so,
I mean, you know,

599
00:30:53,352 --> 00:30:54,562
you don't have
to think about it.

600
00:30:54,586 --> 00:30:57,554
Yeah, but... I mean, I do.

601
00:30:57,623 --> 00:30:59,689
I think about a lot of things.

602
00:30:59,758 --> 00:31:00,957
All right. Okay.

603
00:31:01,025 --> 00:31:02,592
Listen, tell your
mother I'll be by

604
00:31:02,661 --> 00:31:04,594
a little after 6:00. Okay.

605
00:31:16,941 --> 00:31:18,975
If you need any help, I'll
be with you in a minute.

606
00:31:19,043 --> 00:31:20,477
Uh, no, I'll...

607
00:31:20,545 --> 00:31:22,223
I'll know what I'm
looking for when I find it.

608
00:31:22,247 --> 00:31:24,180
Thanks, sir.

609
00:31:25,083 --> 00:31:27,016
♪ ♪

610
00:31:57,048 --> 00:31:58,982
♪ ♪

611
00:32:02,655 --> 00:32:04,454
Is that Tony?

612
00:32:20,606 --> 00:32:22,149
Tony?

613
00:32:22,173 --> 00:32:23,506
Mr. Matlock.

614
00:32:23,575 --> 00:32:25,341
Miss Thomas, hi.

615
00:32:25,411 --> 00:32:27,043
What are you doing?

616
00:32:27,112 --> 00:32:28,912
Uh, nothing. Just, uh...

617
00:32:28,981 --> 00:32:31,081
Are you burying something?

618
00:32:31,149 --> 00:32:32,315
No, no! I was just, uh,

619
00:32:32,384 --> 00:32:34,128
planting some
roses for my mother.

620
00:32:34,152 --> 00:32:35,352
You know, I promised her that

621
00:32:35,387 --> 00:32:39,122
I'd do it and, uh,
but I felt bad that I...

622
00:32:39,191 --> 00:32:40,957
hadn't done it.

623
00:32:41,026 --> 00:32:42,592
That...

624
00:32:42,660 --> 00:32:45,595
looks like the sweater
your mother was wearing

625
00:32:45,663 --> 00:32:47,063
the day Cal died.

626
00:32:47,132 --> 00:32:49,244
No, no, I think you're
mistaken. Yeah, it does.

627
00:32:49,268 --> 00:32:51,828
She was wearing it
at the police station.

628
00:32:52,704 --> 00:32:54,916
There's, there's
something on there.

629
00:32:54,940 --> 00:32:56,106
Give me that.

630
00:32:56,175 --> 00:32:59,176
No, no-no. This...
we have to keep this.

631
00:32:59,244 --> 00:33:00,622
This is evidence. Yeah.

632
00:33:00,646 --> 00:33:03,680
Please, can't we just
make like it doesn't exist?

633
00:33:03,749 --> 00:33:07,417
I wish we could,
Tony, but we can't.

634
00:33:07,486 --> 00:33:11,488
See, I was trying
to help my mother.

635
00:33:11,556 --> 00:33:12,967
And, and now, I've really just

636
00:33:12,991 --> 00:33:15,425
gone and made it worse, huh?

637
00:33:16,328 --> 00:33:19,596
Uh... looks like.

638
00:33:41,186 --> 00:33:43,498
What are you looking for, Ben?

639
00:33:43,522 --> 00:33:45,622
Same thing Tony was looking for.

640
00:33:49,761 --> 00:33:51,894
Yeah...

641
00:33:51,963 --> 00:33:53,574
How will you know
when you find it?

642
00:33:53,598 --> 00:33:55,198
I don't know.

643
00:33:56,535 --> 00:33:58,801
I don't know.

644
00:34:04,142 --> 00:34:05,741
Well, there it is.

645
00:34:07,145 --> 00:34:09,245
What? See?

646
00:34:11,149 --> 00:34:12,415
No, what?

647
00:34:12,484 --> 00:34:14,417
Nothing.

648
00:34:17,523 --> 00:34:19,222
<i>Mr. Matlock?</i>

649
00:34:22,494 --> 00:34:24,494
Call your next witness.

650
00:34:24,563 --> 00:34:27,163
Uh, yes, sir, Your Honor.

651
00:34:30,469 --> 00:34:32,536
Bonnie, we've been
friends for years

652
00:34:32,604 --> 00:34:35,539
and I would never do
anything to hurt you, but...

653
00:34:37,709 --> 00:34:39,443
I have to go ahead with this.

654
00:34:39,511 --> 00:34:41,945
I'm sorry. Oh, Ben.

655
00:34:43,682 --> 00:34:46,282
I call Tony Morgen to the stand.

656
00:34:49,855 --> 00:34:53,690
Now, you have told the police

657
00:34:55,126 --> 00:34:58,060
that on the day your
stepfather was killed

658
00:34:58,129 --> 00:35:02,832
uh, after your swimming
practice, you went home.

659
00:35:02,901 --> 00:35:03,933
Are you sure

660
00:35:04,002 --> 00:35:05,835
you didn't go someplace else?

661
00:35:05,904 --> 00:35:08,204
Positive. I went straight home.

662
00:35:08,273 --> 00:35:10,806
You didn't go by your
stepfather's studio?

663
00:35:10,875 --> 00:35:14,210
Uh, I said I went straight home.

664
00:35:14,279 --> 00:35:15,745
Okay.

665
00:35:15,814 --> 00:35:18,347
Okay, the reason, uh, I asked

666
00:35:18,416 --> 00:35:23,119
was the day after your
step-dad was killed,

667
00:35:23,188 --> 00:35:25,655
I drove over to his studio.

668
00:35:25,723 --> 00:35:27,456
And, uh, the street

669
00:35:27,525 --> 00:35:30,794
in front of the studio
has been resurfaced.

670
00:35:30,862 --> 00:35:34,864
And, uh, my car got
tar splattered on it.

671
00:35:34,933 --> 00:35:37,867
Same thing happened
to Lieutenant Brooks.

672
00:35:37,936 --> 00:35:40,971
And when I saw that tar

673
00:35:41,039 --> 00:35:42,872
had been splattered on your car,

674
00:35:42,940 --> 00:35:46,376
I knew you had been there, too.

675
00:35:46,445 --> 00:35:50,947
So when were you at your
stepfather's studio, Tony?

676
00:35:51,016 --> 00:35:54,017
Um, sometime that week.

677
00:35:54,086 --> 00:35:55,230
I don't know. I don't remember.

678
00:35:55,254 --> 00:35:57,153
Maybe I can narrow
it down for you.

679
00:35:57,222 --> 00:36:04,060
Uh, according to one of
the parking attendants,

680
00:36:04,129 --> 00:36:07,631
uh, Mario Ruiz,

681
00:36:07,699 --> 00:36:11,300
he remembers washing
your car that Sunday.

682
00:36:11,370 --> 00:36:13,970
And the next morning,

683
00:36:14,038 --> 00:36:16,939
you were driving your
stepfather's Porsche.

684
00:36:17,008 --> 00:36:19,142
So that means, the only time

685
00:36:19,210 --> 00:36:22,178
you could've gotten tar
splattered on your car

686
00:36:22,247 --> 00:36:25,581
would be between the
time you left your club

687
00:36:25,651 --> 00:36:26,950
and the next morning

688
00:36:27,019 --> 00:36:29,852
when you were driving
your stepfather's car.

689
00:36:29,921 --> 00:36:31,954
Well, uh, Mario was mistaken,

690
00:36:32,023 --> 00:36:34,135
because he didn't wash
my car that Sunday.

691
00:36:34,159 --> 00:36:36,259
Oh, no, Mario wasn't mistaken.

692
00:36:36,328 --> 00:36:39,028
For a very good reason.

693
00:36:39,097 --> 00:36:41,364
He gave him a five spot.

694
00:36:43,101 --> 00:36:47,304
You tipped him five dollars.

695
00:36:47,372 --> 00:36:50,039
Money means a
lot to you, doesn't it?

696
00:36:50,108 --> 00:36:53,009
Having it, possessing it,
holding it, buying things

697
00:36:53,077 --> 00:36:54,511
with it, showing it...

698
00:36:54,579 --> 00:36:56,446
It makes you feel
important, doesn't it?

699
00:36:56,515 --> 00:36:58,393
I don't see you, uh,
turning your nose up

700
00:36:58,417 --> 00:36:59,794
at money there, Mr. Matlock.

701
00:36:59,818 --> 00:37:02,485
No, no, I like money.

702
00:37:02,554 --> 00:37:04,387
Money's pretty.

703
00:37:04,456 --> 00:37:06,167
It's green, it's
got numbers on it.

704
00:37:06,191 --> 00:37:08,725
Some of them go
way on up yonder.

705
00:37:10,161 --> 00:37:13,363
But you have to
have a strong regard

706
00:37:13,432 --> 00:37:16,800
for what money
can and cannot do.

707
00:37:16,868 --> 00:37:19,068
The trick with money

708
00:37:19,136 --> 00:37:21,270
is to enjoy it.

709
00:37:21,339 --> 00:37:23,840
If you've got it, 'cause
a lot of people don't.

710
00:37:23,908 --> 00:37:25,007
Lot of people.

711
00:37:25,077 --> 00:37:27,977
But if you've got it, enjoy it.

712
00:37:28,046 --> 00:37:30,680
Not need it to the point

713
00:37:30,748 --> 00:37:33,450
that you cannot
stand the thought

714
00:37:33,518 --> 00:37:35,452
of being without it. That's...

715
00:37:37,923 --> 00:37:41,357
How would you
describe your relationship

716
00:37:41,426 --> 00:37:43,927
with your stepfather?

717
00:37:43,995 --> 00:37:45,561
We got along.

718
00:37:46,998 --> 00:37:49,431
Even though your mother's life

719
00:37:49,500 --> 00:37:52,635
was a constant struggle
to try to keep peace

720
00:37:52,704 --> 00:37:54,282
between the two of you?

721
00:37:54,306 --> 00:37:57,406
Uh, no. We were
a very happy family.

722
00:37:58,877 --> 00:38:02,378
Why was he planning to
send you to military school?

723
00:38:02,447 --> 00:38:04,047
He wasn't.

724
00:38:06,785 --> 00:38:09,230
Uh, we, you know,
we had discussed it

725
00:38:09,254 --> 00:38:10,431
and, uh...

726
00:38:10,455 --> 00:38:12,822
he changed his mind

727
00:38:12,890 --> 00:38:14,936
and decided it would
be best if I stayed home.

728
00:38:14,960 --> 00:38:16,892
Oh, I see. I see.

729
00:38:16,962 --> 00:38:18,127
I see.

730
00:38:18,196 --> 00:38:20,463
Uh, your mother,

731
00:38:20,531 --> 00:38:23,399
your mother had a, a housekeeper

732
00:38:23,468 --> 00:38:26,369
named, uh, Shirley
Taylor, didn't she?

733
00:38:26,438 --> 00:38:28,438
Yes. Yeah.

734
00:38:28,473 --> 00:38:29,673
What happened to Miss Taylor?

735
00:38:29,740 --> 00:38:33,109
Uh, Cal fired her for stealing.

736
00:38:33,178 --> 00:38:37,881
Oh. And among the
alleged items that she stole

737
00:38:37,949 --> 00:38:41,050
was an antique gold
lighter, isn't that right?

738
00:38:42,487 --> 00:38:45,121
Maybe, yeah. She
stole a lot of stuff.

739
00:38:45,190 --> 00:38:49,559
B-But you are very familiar
with that antique gold lighter,

740
00:38:49,628 --> 00:38:51,327
aren't you?

741
00:38:51,395 --> 00:38:53,162
I, I don't know.

742
00:38:53,231 --> 00:38:55,498
She was a thief
and I, I don't know.

743
00:38:55,567 --> 00:38:57,233
Maybe she just pawned it, huh?

744
00:38:57,302 --> 00:38:58,534
Could be.

745
00:38:58,603 --> 00:39:00,904
What do you think it was worth?

746
00:39:00,972 --> 00:39:05,174
Lighter, old like
that... I, I don't know.

747
00:39:05,243 --> 00:39:12,148
You know exactly what
it's worth to the penny.

748
00:39:13,284 --> 00:39:14,984
Because you stole it.

749
00:39:15,053 --> 00:39:16,986
And you pawned it.

750
00:39:18,723 --> 00:39:20,523
No, that's not true.

751
00:39:20,592 --> 00:39:21,869
Yes, it is.

752
00:39:21,893 --> 00:39:23,938
And then you framed Shirley

753
00:39:23,962 --> 00:39:26,174
knowing that Cal would fire her.

754
00:39:26,198 --> 00:39:29,599
And that gave you someone
to blame your thefts on.

755
00:39:29,668 --> 00:39:33,703
And you had been stealing
from Cal for a very long time.

756
00:39:33,772 --> 00:39:37,774
<i>And</i> you got rid of
the only other person,

757
00:39:37,842 --> 00:39:41,543
besides the family, who
had heard the arguments

758
00:39:41,612 --> 00:39:43,712
between you and your stepfather.

759
00:39:43,782 --> 00:39:45,514
There were no arguments.

760
00:39:45,584 --> 00:39:49,218
Shirley Taylor can
and will take the stand

761
00:39:50,622 --> 00:39:53,723
and say that you and Cal Ritter,

762
00:39:53,792 --> 00:39:56,125
your stepfather,
fought constantly.

763
00:39:56,194 --> 00:39:58,895
And that you spied on
your mother and him,

764
00:39:58,963 --> 00:40:01,197
and that he intended
to send you away.

765
00:40:01,265 --> 00:40:03,166
My mother would not permit that.

766
00:40:03,234 --> 00:40:04,534
I'm the man of the house.

767
00:40:04,603 --> 00:40:07,036
Not after she
married Cal Ritter.

768
00:40:07,105 --> 00:40:08,716
Cal Ritter was the
man of the house.

769
00:40:08,740 --> 00:40:11,240
Cal Ritter took your
place in the house.

770
00:40:11,309 --> 00:40:13,776
You were no longer

771
00:40:13,845 --> 00:40:16,124
central to your mother's life.

772
00:40:16,148 --> 00:40:18,414
Cal was now taking her time.

773
00:40:18,483 --> 00:40:20,617
Cal was now
taking her attention.

774
00:40:20,685 --> 00:40:23,486
She was buying
him fancy sports cars,

775
00:40:23,555 --> 00:40:27,656
watches... all the things she
should have been buying you.

776
00:40:27,726 --> 00:40:30,026
Things that were
important to you.

777
00:40:30,095 --> 00:40:35,231
She loves you, but now she
had someone else to love.

778
00:40:35,300 --> 00:40:36,632
And he loved her.

779
00:40:36,701 --> 00:40:40,203
And he could take her
into their secret room

780
00:40:40,272 --> 00:40:43,739
and close the door.

781
00:40:43,809 --> 00:40:47,209
Close the door on you.

782
00:40:47,279 --> 00:40:49,812
And what's more,

783
00:40:49,881 --> 00:40:51,926
that same man was planning

784
00:40:51,950 --> 00:40:53,983
to send you away...

785
00:40:54,052 --> 00:40:57,253
Send you away from your home.

786
00:40:57,322 --> 00:41:00,023
From your mother!

787
00:41:01,893 --> 00:41:04,660
Your Honor...

788
00:41:04,729 --> 00:41:05,929
may I speculate for a moment?

789
00:41:05,964 --> 00:41:08,964
Madam D.A.?

790
00:41:09,034 --> 00:41:11,367
I hate it when he does that.

791
00:41:11,436 --> 00:41:14,137
He tells these stories
like he's spinning a web.

792
00:41:14,205 --> 00:41:15,438
Now, where is the proof?

793
00:41:15,507 --> 00:41:17,439
Proof. Proof.

794
00:41:18,510 --> 00:41:19,876
All right, all right.

795
00:41:19,944 --> 00:41:21,689
Your Honor? Go ahead.

796
00:41:21,713 --> 00:41:23,646
Thank you.

797
00:41:26,384 --> 00:41:32,154
That Sunday, when
you left the club...

798
00:41:35,026 --> 00:41:37,960
you didn't go straight home.

799
00:41:38,029 --> 00:41:42,031
You drove out to
your stepfather's studio

800
00:41:42,099 --> 00:41:46,235
and there, the two of
you had a hell of a fight.

801
00:41:46,304 --> 00:41:49,371
Maybe about the military school.

802
00:41:49,440 --> 00:41:51,407
Maybe about the fact
that he had discovered

803
00:41:51,475 --> 00:41:53,742
you were the one who
was stealing and not Shirley.

804
00:41:53,812 --> 00:41:57,280
Maybe he threatened to tell your
mother what he knew about you.

805
00:41:57,348 --> 00:42:02,351
But whatever it was,
you knew he had you cold.

806
00:42:02,420 --> 00:42:04,120
So you picked up something.

807
00:42:04,189 --> 00:42:05,855
Maybe a piece of wood.

808
00:42:05,924 --> 00:42:07,589
And you hit him.

809
00:42:07,658 --> 00:42:09,826
You hit him hard
and you killed him.

810
00:42:09,895 --> 00:42:13,930
And then, to erase your tracks,

811
00:42:13,999 --> 00:42:15,831
you set a fire.

812
00:42:15,901 --> 00:42:18,534
Then, to give
yourself a good alibi,

813
00:42:18,603 --> 00:42:20,203
you set all the clocks back.

814
00:42:20,272 --> 00:42:28,110
And then, in a final
act of disregard,

815
00:42:28,179 --> 00:42:31,414
you took that
antique gold lighter.

816
00:42:31,483 --> 00:42:38,854
The one Cal had found
in a pawn shop where you

817
00:42:38,924 --> 00:42:40,456
had pawned it.

818
00:42:40,525 --> 00:42:42,225
I didn't kill Cal.

819
00:42:44,696 --> 00:42:48,497
I didn't want to
believe you had.

820
00:42:48,566 --> 00:42:51,467
And I wish you hadn't.

821
00:42:51,536 --> 00:42:54,403
But when I saw you burying
your mother's sweater

822
00:42:54,472 --> 00:42:56,505
that convinced me.

823
00:42:56,574 --> 00:42:59,241
There are any number
of flammable materials

824
00:42:59,310 --> 00:43:01,577
that you could've
poured on that sweater.

825
00:43:01,645 --> 00:43:05,080
But you chose paint stripper.

826
00:43:05,150 --> 00:43:09,718
Only the killer could've known

827
00:43:09,787 --> 00:43:13,722
that paint stripper was
used to start that fire.

828
00:43:19,764 --> 00:43:26,402
You tried to incriminate
your own mother.

829
00:43:45,823 --> 00:43:50,126
You remember, uh...
you remember the day

830
00:43:50,194 --> 00:43:54,563
I found you going through
Cal's papers on his desk?

831
00:43:54,632 --> 00:43:55,898
I was helping my mother.

832
00:43:55,967 --> 00:43:58,568
You were looking for
a credit card receipt

833
00:43:58,636 --> 00:44:03,238
that would prove that Cal had
redeemed that antique lighter.

834
00:44:03,308 --> 00:44:04,973
No, I wasn't. And you found it.

835
00:44:05,042 --> 00:44:06,241
Probably burned it.

836
00:44:06,311 --> 00:44:09,779
But you left the folder
with the rest of the bills.

837
00:44:09,847 --> 00:44:12,281
And when I was going
through them later,

838
00:44:12,350 --> 00:44:16,085
I found those
credit card receipts

839
00:44:16,154 --> 00:44:19,956
and they were all there,

840
00:44:20,025 --> 00:44:22,191
except for the month of July.

841
00:44:24,496 --> 00:44:27,763
But the credit card company
was very cooperative.

842
00:44:29,834 --> 00:44:31,600
And they gave me a copy.

843
00:44:34,005 --> 00:44:35,838
And there it is.

844
00:44:35,907 --> 00:44:39,675
July 18.

845
00:44:39,744 --> 00:44:44,781
Cal bought that
antique lighter back

846
00:44:44,849 --> 00:44:48,483
from the Walnut Street Pawn Shop

847
00:44:48,553 --> 00:44:53,822
where you had
pawned it for $1,500.

848
00:44:58,029 --> 00:45:00,296
So...

849
00:45:02,967 --> 00:45:08,337
when Cal was killed,
he had the lighter.

850
00:45:09,740 --> 00:45:11,540
You mother saw it in the studio.

851
00:45:11,609 --> 00:45:13,342
Harry the guard saw it.

852
00:45:13,411 --> 00:45:16,112
But after the fire,

853
00:45:16,180 --> 00:45:17,879
it was not in his
personal effects.

854
00:45:17,948 --> 00:45:20,416
It was not in the studio.

855
00:45:20,485 --> 00:45:23,853
We searched...

856
00:45:23,921 --> 00:45:28,290
200 pawn shops

857
00:45:28,359 --> 00:45:30,960
till we found this lighter.

858
00:45:33,898 --> 00:45:35,998
This is the antique
lighter, isn't it?

859
00:45:37,668 --> 00:45:40,402
With the letter "B" in diamonds?

860
00:45:41,839 --> 00:45:46,542
Only the killer
could've possessed

861
00:45:46,611 --> 00:45:49,111
and pawned this lighter.

862
00:45:51,082 --> 00:45:53,583
You couldn't stand
to leave it, could you?

863
00:45:53,651 --> 00:45:59,788
You couldn't stand not
to steal it one more time.

864
00:45:59,858 --> 00:46:06,762
Not when you could get $1,500.

865
00:46:19,643 --> 00:46:21,577
Give it up, son.

866
00:46:24,482 --> 00:46:29,652
The man from the pawn
shop in Jacksonville

867
00:46:29,721 --> 00:46:31,821
can identify you.

868
00:46:35,993 --> 00:46:37,953
You shouldn't have
married him, Mom.

869
00:46:39,864 --> 00:46:42,108
He was going to send me away.

870
00:46:42,132 --> 00:46:43,377
I had to do it.

871
00:46:43,401 --> 00:46:45,579
You know, I had,
I had no choice.

872
00:46:45,603 --> 00:46:48,604
You understand that.

873
00:46:48,672 --> 00:46:49,839
Don't you?

874
00:47:06,623 --> 00:47:08,390
I'm sorry.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
